---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Failure from '../figures/13-failure.astro';
import mark from '../../public/favicon.svg?raw';
---
<Layout title="keystone" description="keystone unlocks an encrypted disk at startup with no typed passphrase. If the computer is stolen, delete its key set before it next starts, and the disk stays locked.">
  <header class="hero">
    <div class="hero-mark" aria-hidden="true" set:html={mark} />
    <div>
      <p class="hero-eyebrow">disk unlock server</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title">keystone</h1>
      <p class="hero-lede">keystone lets a computer with an encrypted disk unlock it at startup with no one typing a passphrase. At boot the computer gets its part of the key from keystone over the internet. If the computer is stolen, delete its key set before it next starts, and the disk stays locked.</p>
      <div class="hero-cta">
        <a class="cta-primary" href="/setup/">Install</a>
        <a class="cta-secondary" href="/architecture/">Architecture</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="what-it-is">What it is</h2>
    <p class="section-prose">An encrypted disk asks for its passphrase each time the computer starts. Someone at the keyboard types it. A machine with no one there, such as a home server coming back after a power cut, waits at the prompt until someone does.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">keystone supplies the missing half of a second key the computer keeps on the disk, so the disk opens without the passphrase. It is for computers that can reach the internet at boot but have no unlock server on their own network.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">You run your own copy of keystone on Cloudflare, a company that runs small programs on its servers for you. If a computer is stolen, delete its key set. Until you do, anyone who starts that computer where it can reach the internet unlocks the disk.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="how-it-works">How it works</h2>
    <div class="tile-list">
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">1 · bind</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Bind the disk once</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">The disk uses LUKS, the standard Linux disk encryption. clevis, the unlock tool on the computer, adds a second way in tied to a key set: two keys keystone holds under one name.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">2 · boot</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Unlock at startup</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">At startup the initramfs, the small system that runs before the disk opens, asks keystone for its part of the key. With keystone's answer and what the disk stores, the disk opens.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <p class="tile-eyebrow">3 · delete</p>
        <h3 class="tile-headline">Delete a stolen disk's key set</h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">If a computer or disk is stolen, delete its key set. keystone stops answering for that set, so the disk stays locked to whoever took it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="cat-explainer figure-pair">
      <Failure />
      <p>If keystone is offline or past its daily request limit, the disk asks for its passphrase; once a stolen disk's key set is deleted, keystone refuses it and the disk stays locked.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="secrets">Where the secrets are</h2>
    <p class="section-prose">The disk's passphrase and key never leave the computer. The LUKS header, the start of the disk where its unlock slots live, keeps a value that opens the clevis slot only with keystone's private key.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">keystone holds two private keys per key set, as secrets in your Cloudflare account. At boot the computer sends the header value <a href="/architecture/#unlock">blinded</a>, mixed with a fresh random number so keystone never sees it. keystone multiplies it by its private key, and the computer removes the random number to get the key. keystone alone holds nothing that opens a disk.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">Each program version keeps its own copy of the secrets, so <a href="/architecture/#rollback">rolling back</a> past a delete serves the deleted key set again.</p>
    <p class="section-prose">Cloudflare, and anyone who can deploy keystone in your account, can read the key sets. To open a disk they would also need the disk. Treat your offline copy of the key sets like the disks. <a href="/architecture/#trust">Architecture</a> shows how to split trust across two servers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="what-you-need">What you need</h2>
    <ul class="section-prose">
      <li>A Linux computer with a LUKS disk and clevis installed.</li>
      <li>A Cloudflare account and a domain managed there. keystone runs as a Cloudflare Worker, Cloudflare's name for such a program. The free plan works with the smaller key type (<code>--curve P-256</code>); the default key type needs the paid Workers plan.</li>
      <li>A computer with Node.js, git, jq and jose to create and deploy key sets.</li>
      <li>An offline place to keep key sets, such as pass on a machine you control.</li>
      <li>Each disk's passphrase, kept for the day keystone cannot answer.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="next">Where next</h2>
    <div class="tile-grid">
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="/architecture/">Architecture</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">Each mechanism in figures, from binding a disk to deleting a key set.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="/setup/">Install</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">The commands to deploy keystone and bind a first disk.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <h3 class="tile-headline"><a href="https://gitlab.com/dunn.dev/keystone" rel="external">Source</a></h3>
        <p class="tile-prose">Shared as-is: no roadmap, no support promise.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
